#Application State
State of the application after implementing the articles and the most important learnings.

DaylyAI is an Android app (Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, minSdk 24) using MVVM with feature-based packages. It has one feature, Articles, which loads top headlines from the News API. Architecture: articles/data (ArticleData, ArticleApi, ArticleRepository, NetworkModule), articles/presentation (Article, ArticleUIState, ArticleViewModel), articles/ui (ArticleScreen, ArticleUIStateProvider). No domain layer. Stack: Hilt, Retrofit, Moshi, OkHttp, KSP (no kapt), StateFlow, Coil, Timber. Hilt is applied via the version catalog. Kotlin 2.3.10 and KSP 2.3.6 are used for compatibility with AGP 9 built-in Kotlin. DaylyAIApplication initializes Hilt and Timber; NEWS_API_KEY comes from BuildConfig and must be set in gradle.properties. Article.mapArticle() converts ArticleData into Article and formats dates as "Today", "Yesterday", or "X days ago".
Known issues and fixes
The Hilt Gradle plugin is published as com.google.dagger:hilt-android-gradle-plugin; resolving it with the usual plugin id from the catalog can fail. In that case, add it via the root buildscript (e.g. classpath("com.google.dagger:hilt-android-gradle-plugin:2.52")). Use plugin version 2.52 (not 2.52.1; that version does not exist). KSP 2.2.x used the kotlin.sourceSets DSL, which conflicts with AGP 9 built-in Kotlin and leads to "Using kotlin.sourceSets DSL to add Kotlin sources is not allowed" — upgrade KSP to 2.3.6 and Kotlin to 2.3.10. If build failures persist, ensure the app has INTERNET permission and that Timber is initialized in onCreate() of the Application class.
How to work effectively
Run ./gradlew assembleDebug after setting NEWS_API_KEY in gradle.properties. Keep presentation logic in presentation models; use Article.mapArticle() for mapping and formatting, not the ViewModel. Error messages shown to users are generic ("Something went wrong, please try again later"); technical errors should be logged with Timber.e(). For future features, use the same structure: feature/data (data models, API, repository, DI), feature/presentation (UI models, UI state, ViewModel), feature/ui (screens). NetworkModule lives in articles/data and provides OkHttp, Retrofit, Moshi, and ArticleApi. MainActivity is @AndroidEntryPoint and shows ArticleScreen; ArticleViewModel is obtained via hiltViewModel().
